language 0.1.0
module tasks
  uses core, data, cli
  means
    A command-line list of tasks kept in a local tasks.json file: the tasks
    it holds, their priorities, and the ways a person adds and views them.

value task-text : core:text
  means
    task text: the words describing one task.

value tag : core:text
  means
    tag: a word that groups tasks by their source or subject.

enum priority
  high
  normal
  low
  means
    priority: the urgency of a task — high before ordinary work, normal as
    the ordinary case, or low after it.

entity task : data:record
  text     : task-text
  done     : core:boolean
  tags     : tag       many
  priority : priority
  means
    task: one item in the list, with its text, completion mark, tags and
    priority.

requirement R-001
  states
    A newly added [task|task] has [task.priority|priority]
    [priority.normal|normal] unless the person selects another priority.
  because
    Ordinary work is the usual case and must not require an extra option.

requirement R-002
  states
    The `add` command accepts `--priority high`, `--priority normal`, or
    `--priority low` when it creates a [task|task], and stores the selected
    [task.priority|priority].
  because
    A person must be able to set urgency while recording a task.

requirement R-003
  states
    Every [task|task] shown by the `list` command displays its
    [task.priority|priority] as `\[high]`, `\[normal]`, or `\[low]`
    immediately after its completion mark and before its text.
  because
    The priority has to be visible where a person chooses what to do next.

requirement R-004
  states
    The `list` command shows [task|tasks] with
    [priority.high|high] [task.priority|priority] first, then
    [priority.normal|normal], then [priority.low|low], while preserving the
    file order among tasks of each priority.
  because
    Urgent work must rise to the top without making equally urgent tasks
    jump around.

requirement R-005
  states
    The `list` command accepts `--priority high`, `--priority normal`, or
    `--priority low` and shows only [task|tasks] with that
    [task.priority|priority].
  because
    A person sometimes needs to see one urgency level without losing the
    other ways of narrowing the list.

requirement R-006
  states
    A tasks.json file written before [task.priority|priority] existed still
    opens with its task order, text, completion marks and [task.tags|tags]
    unchanged, and each such [task|task] is treated as
    [priority.normal|normal].
  because
    A long-lived list must remain usable after priorities are introduced.

requirement R-007
  states
    The `list` command combines `--priority` selection with `--done`,
    `--open` and `--tag` selection so that a shown [task|task] meets every
    selected condition.
  because
    Combining filters must continue to narrow the same list rather than
    silently discard an existing condition.

requirement R-008
  states
    When `--priority` occurs more than once in `add` or `list`, its last
    value selects the [task.priority|priority].
  because
    Repeated valued options must have one deterministic meaning.

requirement R-009
  states
    A `list` line retains the file-position number, completion mark, text,
    number alignment and [task.tags|tag] representation that it had before
    priorities, and the priority fragment is its only added output.
  because
    Existing scripts and people must keep recognising the list while they
    gain one new piece of information.

requirement R-010
  states
    A task from a pre-priority tasks.json file retains its file position,
    text, completion mark and [task.tags|tags] after a later `add` or
    `done` command saves the file, and that save writes its
    [task.priority|priority] as [priority.normal|normal].
  because
    Opening an old list is not enough if the next ordinary command loses
    data or leaves the new value unspecified.

requirement R-011
  states
    Except for the priority behaviour stated in R-001 through R-010, the
    existing [cli:command|commands], [cli:option|options], successful
    [cli:output-stream|output], error messages, [cli:exit-status|exit
    statuses] and [cli:usage|usage] of the command-line program remain
    unchanged.
  because
    Priorities are an addition to the established tool, not a revision of
    its other behaviour.

exclude X-001
  states
    Editing the [task.priority|priority] of an existing [task|task], due
    dates, projects and coloured list output are not part of this change.
  because
    The change is limited to recording and viewing priorities on newly
    added tasks.
